# Line endings are LF in the worktree on every platform, and therefore in everything built from the
# tracked tree. Without this, `core.autocrlf=true` (the normal Windows Git setting) puts CRLF in the
# worktree AND in `git archive` output, so `make release` on Windows produced an archive that did not
# match the one CI builds from the same commit: 2356 CR bytes in the Makefile alone, 9338880 bytes
# against 9164800, a different SHA-256. An explicit eol attribute overrides core.autocrlf, which is
# what makes the release reproducible across platforms rather than per-checkout. It also fixes
# check-version-drift, check-release-artifacts and check-public-coverage, which compare generated LF
# output against tracked files and so could not pass on a Windows checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Fuzz corpora are BYTE SEEDS, not text. Several deliberately contain CRLF because CRLF is the
# protocol syntax under test (HTTP request and response seeds, PROXY v1 headers): 20 tracked corpus
# files have CR in their committed content. Normalising them would silently change what they test, so
# they are binary as far as Git is concerned and pass through byte for byte.
fuzz/corpus_*/** -text
*.bin -text
